30,2,,status,Shunichi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
31,2,,status,May,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
32,2,,status,Servas,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
33,2,,status,Ryuji,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
34,2,,status,Jonathan,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"The data is clear. A small minority uses the room most frequently, yet the cleanup burden is shared or ignored. We need a structured booking system.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,The physical layout is a problem. We have no privacy; the partition is practically transparent to sound. We are strangers who can hear everything but say nothing.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,May,,"Here's the kicker: we're spending a fortune on outside hire for tiny meetings, and we're wasting hours of our lives walking for lunch because this room isn't a kitchen.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, exactly. We've spent 68,000 yen on external rentals. A professional fit-out for a meeting space is 141,000. It pays for itself in two years of inefficiency.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Servas,,I have found that the walls are thin and the desks are dusty. We are living in a state of sensory clutter that disturbs the mind and the spirit.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Jonathan, privacy is a luxury, but accountability is a necessity. If we make it a dedicated meeting room, we can track who is in there and who cleans it.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Ryuji, it's not about luxury. When we have to say something difficult, we do it in front of everyone. We need a space that allows for vulnerability and honesty.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,May,,"Why are we talking about ""vulnerability"" when we're losing nearly two hours a week just walking for food? If this room becomes a break area, we save time.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,Let's be realistic. A break room is a luxury; a professional meeting space is a business requirement. The external hire costs prove we have a deficit in professional space.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Servas,,"If we make it a break room, the crumbs and noise will only increase, disturbing those of us who work just outside the door. Peace is the priority.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"A break room would only exacerbate the cleaning issue I found. Without a strict system, it will become a dumping ground. A meeting room is easier to regulate.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"A meeting room is just another place for formal talk. I'm arguing for a space where we can actually connect as people, away from the prying ears of the office.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,May,,"@Servas, you're worried about noise, but I'm worried about the physical toll. Walking 25 minutes for lunch is a waste of human energy. Let's make it a kitchen/break area.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, the cost of the kitchen fit-out is 96,000 yen, but it doesn't solve the 68,000 yen annual drain on external rentals. The meeting room is the only rational investment.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Servas,,"@Jonathan, I agree that we need a place for honest talk, but that requires silence. A break room is loud; a meeting room is focused. Let us choose the path of quiet.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,"We have three options: a professional meeting room, a break/kitchen area, or a quiet lounge. Based on the usage tally, a meeting room is the most utilized.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,I will support any option that provides actual acoustic privacy. We cannot continue to be strangers who only speak in public or in formal meetings.,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,May,,I'm sticking to my guns. The time waste is the biggest hidden cost here. A break room with a fridge and microwave saves us hours of walking every single week.,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,"Time is money, May, but direct costs are what we have to sign for. The external hire is a leak we must plug. The meeting room is the most logical choice.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Servas,,"Let us remember that the quality of our work depends on the quality of our environment. A space for focused, quiet collaboration will lift us all.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,3,,status,Shunichi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
